+# Run facts (grounding)
+
+Seed fabro-bfe1 (qualitygate loop-asset tier). 7 stages, 1 visit each, zero retries, first-pass approval. Wall 406 s, active 371 s, **$0.389 total**: planner $0.102 (64 s), implementer $0.228 (228 s), reviewer $0.059 (76 s); script stages 5 s combined. All numbers from run checkpoints/events (seq 78, 232, 296, 306) and the tester/evidence script timings.
+
+---
+
+## 1. Close the gate-ban loophole the planner drove a truck through
+**Evidence:** The brief's criterion (6) told the implementer to end verification with "a direct `nu scripts/qualitygate.nu` run reaching the new tier (expect GATE GREEN)" (checkpoint seq 82). `justfile:128` is literally `nu scripts/qualitygate.nu` — so the planner rewrote the banned `just qualitygate` into its byte-equivalent recipe body, and the implementer ran the full gate (its PASS report: "direct `nu scripts/qualitygate.nu` GATE GREEN", seq 232). Result: **the gate ran three times on one tree** — implementer, tester (4.3 s), reviewer re-ran it again (reviewer journal obs 1, seq 296).
+**Change:** `.fabro/workflows/develop/prompts/planner.md`, step 7 — extend the gate-command rewrite rule from "names `just qualitygate`" to "names the gate command **or its recipe body/equivalent invocation** (per PROJECT_FACTS, the recipe delegates to `nu scripts/qualitygate.nu`)"; permitted substitute stays "gate green via the deterministic tester step".
+**Expected effect:** Eliminates implementer/reviewer redundant gate executions. Free here (4 s, no crates); on any Rust-touching seed it saves a cold compile (8–15 min per the gate measurement cited in `scripts/qualitygate.nu`'s own header) and the risk of a bounce cycle on a self-inflicted red.
+
+## 2. Batch planner reconnaissance and kill the `sd ready` firehose
+**Evidence:** The planner spent 8 sequential tool calls before claiming (seq 31–69: `sd ready` → `fabro_runs_list`+`sd show` → `ls`/`grep justfile` → `grep`+`sed` closeout-smoke → `grep` qualitygate defs → `sed 130,179p` + evidence defs → claim). The `sd ready --limit 200` call returned **200 issues / 27,483 bytes of stdout** (seq 32) to select 1 seed. Planner: 59.8 s inference, $0.102 = **26% of run cost** for zero code.
+**Change:** `.fabro/workflows/develop/prompts/planner.md` steps 1–3 — prescribe ONE batched shell call for the stale-basis probes (`ls` + `grep -n` + `sed -n` combined, as the model already did pairwise at seq 45/51/57/63), and a top-N view of `sd ready` (open seeds fabro-55a7/fabro-c3b4 target exactly this; this run re-proves both).
+**Expected effect:** ~40–60 s and ~$0.06 per planner pass (fabro-55a7's measured basis; this run's numbers are consistent), and a shorter claim-to-dispatch window, which is the window that caused the duplicate-claim races the in-flight check exists to guard.
+
+## 3. Raise `preamble_budget_kb` 24 → 32 — the blob detour happened again
+**Evidence:** The reviewer's preamble rendered the evidence capture as `Output (11.5 KB; full value: /tmp/fabro/runtime/blobs/a38ae5cd…json)` (stage prompt, seq 255) even though 11.5 KB fits the reviewer's own `preamble_inline_max_kb=16` — the **aggregate** 24 KB graph budget demoted it first, forcing a `read_file` detour (reviewer tool_time 4.5 s, `read_file` invoked=true). The graph comment in `workflow.fabro` documents this exact failure at 12 KB; raising to 24 did not fix it.
+**Change:** `.fabro/workflows/develop/workflow.fabro`, graph attr `preamble_budget_kb=24` → `32` (open seed fabro-8d2c).
+**Expected effect:** Evidence arrives inline; the reviewer saves a tool round-trip per review and the run avoids the Verification-blocked → evidence-re-capture → re-review path (one such cycle costs ~80 s + $0.06+, or a wrong rejection as in run 01M0SS23MJ cited in the graph comments).
+
+## 4. Give non-blocking review findings a durable home
+**Evidence:** The reviewer found a real defect — `evidence-smoke.nu` (~line 60) restores cwd with hardcoded `cd /workspace/fabro` instead of capturing `$env.PWD`, so the smoke "would misbehave in a worktree checkout" (reviewer painpoint, seq 296) — and approved anyway, correctly, because the only alternative was a full Changes-requested cycle (~4 min, ~$0.39) for a one-line fix. That finding now lives only in journal prose; worse, the broken smoke is **checked into the gate and runs in every future `just qualitygate`** (the tier the seed just added), so the latent break executes on every run.
+**Change:** `.fabro/workflows/develop/prompts/reviewer.md` Decision section — allow `Approved` with a `review_notes` context key for non-blocking findings (open seed fabro-89dd), and have the improve workflow auto-file non-empty `review_notes` as low-priority seeds.
+**Expected effect:** Known defects stop evaporating at approval; specifically the cwd hardcode gets a tracked fix before someone's host checkout reds the new gate tier.
+
+## 5. Make the brief actually bulleted
+**Evidence:** `planner.md` step 6 mandates "BULLETED acceptance criteria, not prose". The shipped brief is a **~3.5 KB single paragraph** with inline "(1)…(6)" numbering (checkpoint seq 82). The implementer then re-parsed it into its own PASS/FAIL list, and the reviewer re-parsed it again — three parses of one dense paragraph.
+**Change:** `.fabro/workflows/develop/prompts/planner.md` step 6 — require newline-separated `- ` bullets in `current_seed_brief` verbatim (open seed fabro-9e49); optionally enforce via the routing output schema.
+**Expected effect:** Cheaper, less error-prone criterion checking for implementer and reviewer (both prompts promise "bullets are checked item-by-item"); removes a misparse class that has historically produced reviewer ping-pong.
+
+## 6. Scope per-stage memory (AGENTS.md is loaded whole into all three agents)
+**Evidence:** `agent.memory.loaded` shows AGENTS.md at **24,605 bytes loaded identically into planner (seq 23), implementer (seq 88), and reviewer** (~6.0–6.5 k tokens each in the memory category of every context breakdown). Most of it is Rust build/test guidance irrelevant to the planner, which writes no Rust.
+**Change:** Engine per-node memory scoping (open seed fabro-9588), or split AGENTS.md so agent stages load a role-filtered section.
+**Expected effect:** ~5 k fewer input tokens per non-Rust stage and faster first output; mostly cache-read cost today, so moderate — but it compounds across every run and every cycle visit.
+
+## 7. Tighten the reviewer's gate re-run trigger
+**Evidence:** The reviewer's preamble already contained the tester's full green output including the new tier executing (`== checking loop-asset scripts == / loop-asset scripts green / GATE GREEN`, seq 255), yet it "re-ran the smoke standalone and the full qualitygate.nu directly" (journal obs 1). Permitted by policy ("when you doubt the gate") but nothing was doubted — it was routine re-verification.
+**Change:** `.fabro/workflows/develop/prompts/reviewer.md` — gate re-runs only when the capture contradicts the tester section or the seed targets the gate itself; otherwise judge from the inline tester output.
+**Expected effect:** One fewer full-gate execution per review; negligible this run (4 s), but on Rust seeds each avoided re-run is a cold compile measured in minutes.
+
+## 8. (Low) Batch checkpoint metadata snapshots
+**Evidence:** Metadata snapshots cost 2.16 s (init, seq 15) and 1.99 s (planner checkpoint, seq 81) per stage boundary; wall 406 s vs active 371 s leaves ~35 s (8.6%) of inter-stage overhead including pushes.
+**Change:** Engine: skip snapshots on non-agent stages / batch pushes at exit boundaries (open seeds fabro-c2ca, fabro-652d).
+**Expected effect:** ~20–30 s off every run's wall time; no quality change.
+
